Description: Add Depth: an element's depth from its style, fitted into the limits
 .
 Depth::elementDepth() gives the depth an element declares, or else the
 one its shadow gives it, following CSS Backgrounds 3 §6.1: an outer
 shadow is cast as if the box were lifted above the canvas, so it raises
 the element one step, half of the popped limit; an inset shadow is cast
 as if the box were cut out of the canvas, so it sinks it. Without either
 the element lies on what it is drawn on. A declared depth always wins,
 so a style without shadows keeps the depths it declares.
 .
 DepthTree holds the elements of one surface. Each stands on what it lies
 on, and when a stack would pass a limit every depth on that side is
 scaled down until the farthest sits at the limit, the way Stereo KWin
 fits a sub-menu over its menu (menu +2, sub-menu +4 at a popped limit of
 4). Elements flush with the output's edge never come nearer than the
 screen, and a branch can lie flat, as a window's content does under a
 modal overlay.
 .
 parallax() and eyeShift() turn a depth into whole pixels with Stereo
 KWin's own rounding, from the user's limits in pixels of a view 1920
 wide, and DepthMotion moves between depths with its motion: about 80 ms
 towards the viewer, 150 ms away, eased out, at the animation speed.
